smashnet - Amiga SANA-II device driver for host-exported network
----------------------------------------------------------------

The smashnet.device is an Amiga SANA-II ethernet device driver which
is compatile with Roadshow and other Amiga TCP/IP network stacks.
It requires Kicksmash and a USB host to be connected, and hostsmash
running with the "-n" network option.

There are two ways to instantiate the smashnet.device in memory.
One is to run the smashnet program, which will create the device and
also report diagnostic information as the driver executes. If you
need to shut down the device, you can press ^C to signal it to stop,
or use the "Status" command and run "Break" to signal it.

The second way is to install smashnet.device in your Devs:Networks
directory. This is the common way that SANA-II devices are loaded on
the Amiga. For Roadshow to automatically start the device, you will
need to create Devs:NetInterfaces/smashnet as a text file which
describes options for smashnet.device. A simple file is provided
in the Kicksmash distribution:
    Copy smashnet.netif Devs:NetInterfaces/smashnet

Start hostsmash with the -n option. Example:
    ./hostsmash -d /dev/ttyACM0 -n

You may also combine networking with file service:
    ./hostsmash -d /dev/ttyACM0 -n -M amiga: ../amiga

hostsmash supports Linux, MacOS, and Windows. In order to support
networking, the host-side implementation is different for each operating
system. The hostsmash utility calls out to hostsmash_netif on each
platform to act as a buffered virtual interface to the physical network.
All operating systems require elevated privileges for the program which
is able to interact directly with the network.

Linux
-----
Under Linux, hostsmash_netif will use pkexec to authenticate and
re-execute itself. You must enter a password to pkexec when hostsmash
starts the network interface program. As an alternative to entering a
password each time, you can set make this executable setuid root.
Example:
    sudo chown root objs.*\/hostsmash_netif
    sudo chmod 4755 objs.*\/hostsmash_netif

or, you can specifically give it network permission:
    sudo setcap cap_dac_override,cap_net_admin+ep objs.*\/hostsmash_netif

The Linux version will use macvtap to create a psuedo-network device
named "amiga-vtap0" which is bridged to your default network interface.
This device has a generated MAC address which is separate from your
default network interface, so that your DHCP server can hand a unique
MAC address to your Amiga.

MacOS
-----
MacOS uses a native administrator-privilege prompt (osascript) to
elevate permissions. It also requires a password to be entered. I've
not experimented with using setuid on MacOS, but imagine that would
work similar to Linux.

The equivalent of macvtap on MacOS is vmnet, but this requires special
entitlement from Apple, so BFP is used instead. MacOS support in
hostsmash is not well-tested, and may end up using the host's MAC for
network access of the Amiga. The solution is to create a virtual
MAC address and record that on disk "virtual_mac.txt", similar to how
the Windows implementation does this. This has not yet been implemented
for MacOS.

Windows
-------
The Windows implementation of hostsmash_netif uses UAC to boost
privilege of a called program. UAC authenticates before starting
hostsmash_netif, but due to how Windows stdin/stdout works, this
escalation must be done in the calling hostsmash program rather than
in hostsmash_netif. If you choose to run hostsmash in an Administrator
CMD prompt, then UAC authentication will be skipped.

Windows has no macvtap equivalent. However, there is more than one
means for hostsmash_netif on Windows to implement the virtual network
device. Both require a driver.
One is using Npcap:
    https://npcap.com/
and the other is using TAP:
    https://openvpn.net/community/

The TAP interface creates a private virtual network interface which
uses network address translation (NAT) to bridge to the default network.
This method does not currently work for an unknown reason. The Amiga is
not able to acquire an address via DHCP.

For now, only the Npcap interface is supported. Like the MacOS BFP,
Npcap implements an interface which shares the hardware with the default
network interface. Because of this, it likely will not work on Wifi
because of how Wifi network security works. The hostsmash_netif Npcap
implementation does generate a custom MAC for the Amiga. It gets saved
to disk as "virtual_mac.txt" for reuse.

All platforms
-------------
When you abort hostsmash, it should trigger the hostsmash_netif program
to clean up and exist. The virtual network interfaces should be
automatically removed when hostsmash_netif exits.
